Future of Cyber Law:
  Hosted by Pavan Duggal, this seminar talks about the changing nature of cyberlaw since the creation of the internet, and the potential changes that still need to be
  made. Here are some notes taken from the seminar:
  Utah was the first state to create cyber law
  Current cyber law encapsulates almost everything to do with online/ digital activities
  The increase in cyber terrorism required much stronger international co-operation
  The quantity of cyber breaches kept increasing month-to-month, and cybersecurity became very importants for stakeholders and companies
  At the time of the video, there was no one agreement on what constitutes internet restrictions
  Expected that by 2020, 20 billion devices will be connected to the internet
  Tau browser hides users behind layers to protect anonymity
    Some users want to use this darknet for legitimate reasons, like not being spied on/ tracked by other users
    The darknet became the place for illegal activities because of this anonymity
      Does this mean that no one can have anonymity on the darknet because of the illegal activities
What is your legal right if cloud data is breached
Source:
  TEDx Talks. (2016, June 17). Future of cyber law | Pavan Duggal | TEDxFORESchool [Video]. YouTube. https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9hXHm3alzDs
Data Privacy and Consent:
  Hosted by Fred Cate in November 2019, this seminar is about the problems, and possible fixes, with consent in relation to data access by companies. Here are some
  notes taken from the seminar:
  Lots of the data on us online has been entered on purpose, in photos on social media
  2017 (New York Times)- A company you've never heard of, engages in 50 trillion personal data transactions a year
  Modern privacy laws were created in the 1990s
  Privacy is the claim of individuals, groups or institutions to determine for themselves, when, how and to what extent information about themselves is communicated to
  others (Dr Alan Weston)
    Used by every country
  Complexity of privacy notices
    Often passed by
  Inaccessible privacy notices
  Consent is ineffective
  Consent is non-optional (illusionary)
  Burden on individuals
  Choice often serves as a disservice
    President, criminals- the consent is often overridden and acknowledged as ineffectual
  Lousy privacy protection
  Less focus on consent and more on the stewardship of data
  Might think more on the things we think should be able to be done with data
  Think more about redress- something will always go wrong
  When we do ask for consent, make it meaningful and timely
Source:
  TEDx Talks. (2020, January 17). Data Privacy and Consent | Fred Cate | TEDxIndianaUniversity [Video]. YouTube. https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2iPDpV8ojHA
